# Seed parameters for one cluster. Copy to `.env` and fill in.
# `just bootstrap` loads this file; anything missing fails the render before the
# cluster is touched.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TOPOLOGY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The only value that differs between clusters. Selects clusters/<name>/ in the
# gitops repository, e.g. master, dev, staging, acme-prod.
TARGET_CLUSTER_NAME=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GITOPS REPOSITORY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# HTTPS clone URL of the gitops repository, e.g. https://github.com/acme/gitops.
# HTTPS and not SSH: the credential below is a GitHub App, which authenticates
# git over HTTP.
GITOPS_REPO_URL=
# Optional; defaults to `master`.
GITOPS_TARGET_REVISION=master

# Argo CD reads the repository as a GitHub App, per
# https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/user-guide/private-repositories/#github-app-credential
# Not the same thing as the OAuth App in the ARGO CD section below. That one
# signs humans in to the UI; this one is the identity Argo CD itself clones
# with. They are registered separately, on different pages, and share nothing.
#
# Register it on the organisation that owns the gitops repository: "Click Your
# organizations." > "To the right of the organization, click Settings." > "In
# the left sidebar, click Developer settings." > "In the left sidebar, click
# GitHub Apps." > "Click New GitHub App." —
# https://docs.github.com/en/apps/creating-github-apps/setting-up-a-github-app/creating-a-github-app
# Set "Where can this GitHub App be installed?" to "Only on this account", and
# deselect the webhook's "Active" checkbox: the app only ever clones, so it
# needs no callback and no events.
#
# Permissions. Argo CD asks for exactly one: "Ensure your application has at
# least `Read-only` permissions to the `Contents` of the repository. This is the
# minimum requirement." —
# https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/user-guide/private-repositories/#github-app-credential
# GitHub names the same one from its side: "If you want your app to use an
# installation or user access token to authenticate for HTTP-based Git access,
# you should request the `Contents` repository permission." —
# https://docs.github.com/en/apps/creating-github-apps/registering-a-github-app/choosing-permissions-for-a-github-app
# So the seed needs exactly one: Repository permissions > Contents: Read-only.
# Argo CD only ever clones the gitops repository with this credential; it never
# writes to it, and nothing about bootstrapping a cluster needs more.
#
# The registration form also offers a Metadata permission, which GitHub sets for
# itself. Neither page above states that it is mandatory, and this file does not
# assert that it is: leave it at whatever GitHub selects and do not lower it.
#
# Beyond the minimum, no single page enumerates the set. Argo CD's feature pages
# name no permissions at all, so each entry below pairs the Argo CD page that
# says what a feature calls with GitHub's endpoint reference,
# https://docs.github.com/en/rest/authentication/permissions-required-for-github-apps
# which lists every REST endpoint under the permission and access level it
# requires. Grant one only when you switch that feature on, and only if you
# reuse this app for it.
#
#   * ApplicationSet Pull Request generator — Pull requests: Read-only.
#     It "uses the API of an SCMaaS provider (GitHub, Gitea, or Bitbucket
#     Server) to automatically discover open pull requests within a repository"
#     and authenticates with "A `Secret` name containing a GitHub App secret in
#     repo-creds format", which is this Secret's format —
#     https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/operator-manual/applicationset/Generators-Pull-Request/
#     That page names no permission. The call it must make,
#     `GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls`, is listed in the reference under
#     Repository permissions for "Pull requests" at access `read`.
#
#   * ApplicationSet SCM Provider generator — nothing beyond the baseline could
#     be established. It "uses the GitHub API to scan an organization in either
#     github.com or GitHub Enterprise" —
#     https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/operator-manual/applicationset/Generators-SCM-Provider/
#     and its scan, `GET /orgs/{org}/repos`, is listed under Metadata at access
#     `read` — the Metadata permission just described, left as GitHub sets it.
#     Filtering repositories by file reads
#     `GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/contents/{path}`, listed under Contents at
#     access `read`, which is already granted.
#
#   * Notifications to GitHub — depends on what you send, and Argo CD says only
#     "Change repository permissions to enable write commit statuses and/or
#     deployments and/or pull requests comments" —
#     https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/operator-manual/notifications/services/github/
#     In the reference those three are
#     `POST /repos/{owner}/{repo}/statuses/{sha}` under Commit statuses at
#     access `write`;
#     `POST /repos/{owner}/{repo}/deployments` under Deployments at access
#     `write`; and `POST /repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/{issue_number}/comments`,
#     which is listed under both Issues and Pull requests at access `write` and
#     flagged there as needing additional permissions. Which of those two
#     suffices for a comment is not something these pages settle — verify it
#     against the notifications you configure rather than granting both.
#
#   * Anything that writes to the gitops repository through this app —
#     Contents: Read and write, because
#     `PUT /repos/{owner}/{repo}/contents/{path}` is listed under Contents at
#     access `write`. Argo CD itself never writes.
#     For a tool that pushes with git instead of the contents API —
#     argocd-image-updater's git write-back is the common one — no page was
#     found stating the level git push requires, so confirm it before assuming
#     Read-only is enough or that write is.
#
#   * GitHub Enterprise Server is not a permission but an extra Secret field,
#     githubAppEnterpriseBaseUrl — see bootstrap/repo-secret.yaml.
#
# Then install the app and give it the repository: on the same GitHub Apps page,
# Edit the app > Install App > Install it on the account > Only select
# repositories > the gitops repository —
# https://docs.github.com/en/apps/using-github-apps/installing-your-own-github-app
# Note that "The app will always have at least read-only access to all public
# repositories on GitHub", so this narrows private access only.

# The app's numeric App ID. "You can also find the app ID on the settings page
# for your app." — and mind that "The app ID is different from the client ID." —
# https://docs.github.com/en/apps/creating-github-apps/authenticating-with-a-github-app/generating-an-installation-access-token-for-a-github-app
# The Client ID sits next to it on that page and is not numeric; pasting it here
# yields a Secret the cluster accepts and Argo CD cannot authenticate with, so
# bootstrap refuses anything that is not all digits.
GITOPS_REPO_GITHUB_APP_ID=

# The numeric ID of *this installation* of the app, which is a different object
# from the app and has a different ID. GitHub documents reading it from a
# webhook payload, or from the REST API — `GET /orgs/{org}/installation`,
# `GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/installation`, `GET /app/installations` —
# https://docs.github.com/en/apps/creating-github-apps/authenticating-with-a-github-app/generating-an-installation-access-token-for-a-github-app
# Those calls need the app's own JWT, so in practice you will read it off the
# installation's settings URL instead,
# https://github.com/organizations/<org>/settings/installations/<id>. That works
# but is not something the documentation promises; the API is the answer if the
# URL ever stops carrying it.
#
# Required here by choice, not by Argo CD. Of the CLI equivalent Argo CD says
# "The `--github-app-installation-id` flag is optional. If omitted, Argo CD will
# automatically discover the installation ID based on the repository's
# organization." —
# https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/user-guide/private-repositories/#github-app-credential
# That sentence is about the flag; nothing found says the Secret field may be
# omitted. Setting it explicitly costs one lookup and removes the discovery step
# from the bootstrap path, so this seed asks for it.
GITOPS_REPO_GITHUB_APP_INSTALLATION_ID=

# Path to the app's private key file — the path, not the key. Generate the key
# on the app's own settings page, under Private keys > Generate a private key:
# "You will see a private key in PEM format downloaded to your computer." GitHub
# keeps only the public half — "Make sure to store this file because GitHub only
# stores the public portion of the key" — so that download is the only copy —
# https://docs.github.com/en/apps/creating-github-apps/authenticating-with-a-github-app/managing-private-keys-for-github-apps
#
# A path rather than the key itself, because a PEM is multi-line and the road
# from here to the Secret is not. Even where a quoted multi-line value survives
# dotenv parsing, `envsubst` expands into YAML that it does not parse: the
# second and every later line of the PEM would land at column zero and the
# manifest would stop parsing. `just bootstrap` reads the file and encodes it to
# a single base64 line instead, so the exact bytes reach the Secret and the key
# is never re-typed, re-quoted or re-indented on the way.
#
# Give an absolute path outside this repository, e.g.
# /home/you/.secrets/acme-gitops-app.pem. It is read by a shell, not expanded by
# one, so `~` is a literal character here and will not resolve to your home
# directory.
GITOPS_REPO_GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ARGO CD
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Hostname Argo CD will answer on once the gitops repository delivers ingress.
# Also the SSO redirect host, so it must match the OAuth App callback below.
# This is a hostname you own DNS for, not issued by Argo CD or GitHub; it maps
# to `global.domain` in the argo-cd chart —
# https://github.com/argoproj/argo-helm/blob/main/charts/argo-cd/values.yaml
ARGO_HOST=

# OIDC client for the Argo CD UI. Today this is the GitHub OAuth App registered
# for this cluster, with callback https://${ARGO_HOST}/api/dex/callback. An
# OAuth App is a separate registration from the GitHub App in the GITOPS
# REPOSITORY section above, on a separate page; this one signs users in, that
# one clones the repository, and neither can stand in for the other.
# Register the app under GitHub Settings > Developer settings > OAuth Apps
# (https://docs.github.com/en/apps/oauth-apps/building-oauth-apps/creating-an-oauth-app),
# setting Authorization callback URL to the value above. OIDC_CLIENT_ID is
# shown on the app's page once created; click "Generate a new client secret"
# to get OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET.
OIDC_CLIENT_ID=
OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Optional. GitHub organisation allowed to sign in, e.g. acme-inc — the org's
# slug as it appears in https://github.com/<org>. Leave empty and any GitHub
# account can reach the Argo CD login — they get no permissions, because
# default RBAC grants none, but set this to keep the door narrower. Becomes
# the `orgs` entry of the Dex GitHub connector — see
# https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/operator-manual/user-management/
# Only applies while the bundled Dex is the issuer.
GITHUB_ORG=

# Leave empty to use the Dex bundled in the argo-cd chart as the issuer, per
# https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/operator-manual/user-management/
# Set it to point at a central Dex broker instead; the bundled Dex then stays off
# and OIDC_CLIENT_ID/SECRET become that broker's client credentials.
OIDC_ISSUER_URL=
